1684:def assert_allclose(actual, desired, rtol=1e-7, atol=0, equal_nan=True,
1685-                    err_msg='', verbose=True, *, strict=False):
1686-    """
1687-    Raises an AssertionError if two objects are not equal up to desired
1688-    tolerance.
1689-
1690-    Given two array_like objects, check that their shapes and all elements
1691-    are equal (but see the Notes for the special handling of a scalar). An
1692-    exception is raised if the shapes mismatch or any values conflict. In
1693-    contrast to the standard usage in numpy, NaNs are compared like numbers,
1694-    no assertion is raised if both objects have NaNs in the same positions.
1695-
1696-    The test is equivalent to ``allclose(actual, desired, rtol, atol)``,
1697-    except that it is stricter: it doesn't broadcast its operands, and has
1698-    tighter default tolerance values. It compares the difference between
1699-    `actual` and `desired` to ``atol + rtol * abs(desired)``.
1700-
1701-    Parameters
1702-    ----------
1703-    actual : array_like
1704-        Array obtained.
1705-    desired : array_like
1706-        Array desired.
1707-    rtol : float, optional
1708-        Relative tolerance.
1709-    atol : float | np.timedelta64, optional
1710-        Absolute tolerance.
1711-    equal_nan : bool, optional.
1712-        If True, NaNs will compare equal.
1713-    err_msg : str, optional
1714-        The error message to be printed in case of failure.
1715-    verbose : bool, optional
1716-        If True, the conflicting values are appended to the error message.
1717-    strict : bool, optional
1718-        If True, raise an ``AssertionError`` when either the shape or the data
1719-        type of the arguments does not match. The special handling of scalars
1720-        mentioned in the Notes section is disabled.
1721-
1722-        .. versionadded:: 2.0.0
1723-
1724-    Raises
1725-    ------
1726-    AssertionError
1727-        If actual and desired are not equal up to specified precision.
1728-
1729-    See Also
1730-    --------
1731-    assert_array_almost_equal_nulp, assert_array_max_ulp
1732-
1733-    Notes
1734-    -----
1735-    When one of `actual` and `desired` is a scalar and the other is array_like, the
1736-    function performs the comparison as if the scalar were broadcasted to the shape
1737-    of the array. Note that empty arrays are therefore considered equal to scalars.
1738-    This behaviour can be disabled by setting ``strict==True``.
1739-
1740-    Examples
1741-    --------
1742-    >>> x = [1e-5, 1e-3, 1e-1]
1743-    >>> y = np.arccos(np.cos(x))
1744-    >>> np.testing.assert_allclose(x, y, rtol=1e-5, atol=0)
