414:Using missing and filling values
415---------------------------------
416-
417:Some entries may be missing in the dataset we are trying to import.  In a
418-previous example, we used a converter to transform an empty string into a
419-float.  However, user-defined converters may rapidly become cumbersome to
420-manage.
421-
422-The :func:`~numpy.genfromtxt` function provides two other complementary
423:mechanisms: the ``missing_values`` argument is used to recognize
424:missing data and a second argument, ``filling_values``, is used to
425:process these missing data.
426-
427:``missing_values``
428-------------------
429-
430:By default, any empty string is marked as missing.  We can also consider
431:more complex strings, such as ``"N/A"`` or ``"???"`` to represent missing
432:or invalid data.  The ``missing_values`` argument accepts three kinds
433-of values:
434-
435-a string or a comma-separated string
436:   This string will be used as the marker for missing data for all the
437-   columns
438-a sequence of strings
439-   In that case, each item is associated to a column, in order.
440-a dictionary
441-   Values of the dictionary are strings or sequence of strings.  The
--
448-------------------
449-
450:We know how to recognize missing data, but we still need to provide a value
451:for these missing entries.  By default, this value is determined from the
452-expected dtype according to this table:
453-
454-=============  ==============
455-Expected type  Default
456-=============  ==============
--
462-=============  ==============
463-
464:We can get a finer control on the conversion of missing values with the
465-``filling_values`` optional argument.  Like
466:``missing_values``, this argument accepts different kind of values:
467-
468-a single value
469-   This will be the default for all columns
470-a sequence of values
471-   Each entry will be the default for the corresponding column
